  "prompt_text": "The figure shows the three-dimensional shape of the human hippocampal formation and how its internal subfields change along the front-to-back axis, especially in the hooked anterior region called the uncus. The large panel on the left outlines the overall curved structure, with the uncus highlighted at the anterior, medial end and the dentate gyrus and hippocampal body labeled within the longer tail-like portion.\n\nThe smaller schematic sections on the right represent slices taken through different levels of this curve. Together they illustrate that the hippocampus is not uniform: the arrangement of dentate gyrus, CA fields, subiculum, prosubiculum, presubiculum/parasubiculum, entorhinal cortex, HATA, and fimbria shifts markedly from the amygdala-adjacent anterior end into the more typical hippocampal body. In the most anterior sections, the uncus appears closely related to the amygdala and the subfields are tightly folded. Moving through the intermediate and posterior slices, the familiar hippocampal arch emerges, with dentate gyrus and CA regions becoming more clearly organized. The final section suggests additional curvature and folding in the uncal region, emphasizing the complex topology of the anterior hippocampus compared with the straighter body.",
